1. Purpose

This document defines the functional requirements for AlertDesk. These requirements describe the functions the service must provide based on the current client brief and configuration-driven workflow requirement.

2. Functional Requirements

ID

Function Requirement

FR-01

Create ticket

The system shall allow an authorized analyst to create a security ticket.

FR-02

Ticket information

The system shall allow a ticket to contain a title, description, and severity.

FR-03

Severity selection

The system shall allow ticket severity to be selected from the values defined by the client specification.

FR-04

List tickets

The system shall allow an authorized analyst to list/retrieve security tickets.

FR-05

Get ticket

The system shall allow an authorized analyst to retrieve the details of an individual ticket.

FR-06

Assign ticket

The system shall allow an authorized analyst to assign a ticket to an analyst identifier, such as an email address or handle.

FR-07

Change ticket status

The system shall allow only authorized Lead and Admin roles to change a ticket status.

FR-08

Validate status transition

The system shall reject a ticket-status change when the transition is not defined as legal in the client specification.

FR-09

Configuration-driven workflow

The system shall obtain ticket statuses, roles, severity values, and legal status transitions from the client JSON specification rather than hardcoding them in Python.

FR-10

Role-based authorization

The system shall enforce permissions according to the authenticated user’s role.

FR-11

Audit ticket creation

The system shall record ticket-creation actions in an append-only audit trail.

FR-12

Audit assignment

The system shall record ticket-assignment actions in an append-only audit trail.

FR-13

Audit status transition

The system shall record ticket-status transitions in an append-only audit trail.

FR-14

Audit actor and time

The system shall record who performed an audited action and when it occurred.

FR-15

API service

The system shall expose the ticket functions through a web service/API rather than a CLI-only interface.

FR-16

OpenAPI documentation

The running application shall expose OpenAPI documentation describing the available API operations and schemas.

3. Role Permissions

Role         Create        List/Get     Assign      Change status

Analyst      Yes           Yes           Yes           No

Lead         Yes           Yes           Yes          Yes

Admin        Yes           Yes           Yes           Yes

4. Current Sample Workflow

The current sample workflow is:

New → Triaged → Assigned → Resolved → Closed

The application shall not allow arbitrary status jumps. The legal transitions must be read from the JSON specification.

5. Acceptance Criteria

.Area

.Acceptance criterion

.Ticket creation

An authorized analyst can create a ticket with a title, description, and a valid client-defined severity.

Ticket retrieval

An authorized analyst can list tickets and retrieve an individual ticket.

Assignment

An authorized analyst can assign a ticket to an analyst identifier.

Status authorization

An Analyst cannot change status; a Lead or Admin can change status when otherwise valid.

Transition validation

An attempted illegal status transition is rejected.

Audit trail

Creating, assigning, or transitioning a ticket creates an append-only audit record identifying the actor and time.

Configuration

Changing the client JSON specification can change supported statuses/roles/transitions without hardcoding those values in Python.

OpenAPI

The running service exposes its API contract through OpenAPI documentation.

6. Functional Scope Exclusions

Slack integration

SIEM connectors

Single Sign-On (SSO)

Multi-region operation

7. Important Implementation Rule

The client specification is the source of truth for configurable workflow elements. Application code and tests should use the current specification rather than duplicating status or role values as hardcoded Python enums.

8. Summary

AlertDesk must provide a service for creating, viewing, assigning, and progressing security tickets. It must enforce role permissions, reject illegal status transitions, maintain an append-only audit trail, and expose OpenAPI documentation. The workflow and role rules must remain configurable through the client specification.
